WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.148
Day 31 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.

00:00:04.698 --> 00:00:09.658
Capstone: the full AI quality pipeline and your 2027 roadmap.

00:00:10.208 --> 00:00:14.653
The previous 30 days tied into a single pipeline, a 12-point checklist to

00:00:14.653 --> 00:00:17.757
self-assess and a 90-day plan with public evidence.

00:00:19.057 --> 00:00:23.999
For thirty days you saw separate pieces: a rule for delegating, an eval, a

00:00:23.999 --> 00:00:27.605
configured agent, a threat model, a load test, a gate.

00:00:27.905 --> 00:00:30.148
Each solves a concrete problem.

00:00:30.448 --> 00:00:34.877
The greater value appears when they connect, because that's where they stop being

00:00:34.877 --> 00:00:38.485
experiments and become a quality system for software that uses AI.

00:00:39.785 --> 00:00:42.086
This last day adds no tools.

00:00:42.386 --> 00:00:46.714
It assembles the full map, asks you for an honest assessment of what you really have

00:00:46.714 --> 00:00:49.239
working and helps you choose what to build first.

00:00:49.539 --> 00:00:53.152
The series doesn't expect you to implement all twelve pieces.

00:00:53.452 --> 00:00:58.221
It expects you to know which ones matter to your product, in what order, and to be

00:00:58.221 --> 00:01:01.477
able to show evidence of progress three months from now.

00:01:02.777 --> 00:01:04.475
To follow this guide you need:

00:01:05.025 --> 00:01:08.697
The qa-ai-lab repo with whatever you built during the series.

00:01:09.247 --> 00:01:11.351
The skills matrix from day 2.

00:01:12.651 --> 00:01:14.139
12-piece checklist.

00:01:14.689 --> 00:01:19.359
Delegation rule: verifiable, cheap to review, reversible (day 1).

00:01:19.909 --> 00:01:23.824
Repo with rules for agents, tools and permissions (day 9).

00:01:24.374 --> 00:01:30.310
Tests written by people, implementation by agents, with guardrails (days 13 and 14).

00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:34.972
Playwright agents with plan and diff review (days 10 and 11).

00:01:35.522 --> 00:01:38.393
Eval harness with a versioned prompt (day 8).

00:01:38.943 --> 00:01:43.253
Pinned model versions and a calibrated judge (days 15 and 16).

00:01:43.803 --> 00:01:48.205
Golden dataset with dev, test and regression (day 17).

00:01:48.755 --> 00:01:53.390
Repeated runs, ranges and cost per call (days 18 and 19).

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:57.066
Prompt regression before every change (day 20).

00:01:57.616 --> 00:02:02.042
Threat model, red teaming and injection tests (days 22 to 24).

00:02:02.592 --> 00:02:06.983
Performance targets and tests with token metrics (days 25 to 27).

00:02:07.534 --> 00:02:12.563
CI gates, reproducible infra and observability (days 28 to 30).

00:02:13.863 --> 00:02:14.899
Step 1.

00:02:15.199 --> 00:02:16.363
The full journey.

00:02:16.913 --> 00:02:21.939
Walk the diagram end to end: a change enters through the repo, produced by an agent

00:02:21.939 --> 00:02:27.025
or a person, and goes through tests, evals, security and performance before reaching

00:02:27.025 --> 00:02:29.327
production, where measuring continues.

00:02:29.877 --> 00:02:32.433
Follow a change end to end on the diagram.

00:02:32.733 --> 00:02:37.273
It enters through the repository, written by a person or by an agent working with

00:02:37.273 --> 00:02:38.506
rules and permissions.

00:02:38.806 --> 00:02:42.164
It goes through deterministic tests and guardrails.

00:02:42.464 --> 00:02:47.210
If it touches anything related to a model, it goes through evals with their dataset

00:02:47.210 --> 00:02:48.411
and calibrated judge.

00:02:48.711 --> 00:02:51.767
Before release, through security and performance.

00:02:52.067 --> 00:02:56.764
In production it stays measured with traces and online evals, and every failure

00:02:56.764 --> 00:02:58.548
returns to the regression set.

00:02:58.848 --> 00:03:02.670
The closed loop is what makes the system improve with use.

00:03:03.970 --> 00:03:05.169
Step 2.

00:03:05.469 --> 00:03:07.108
An honest checklist.

00:03:07.659 --> 00:03:09.739
Fill in the checklist honestly.

00:03:10.039 --> 00:03:14.071
"I saw it in a video" doesn't count; what runs in a repo does.

00:03:14.621 --> 00:03:19.305
Fill in the twelve-piece checklist with a strict rule: a piece counts only if it

00:03:19.305 --> 00:03:21.705
runs in a repository and someone uses it.

00:03:22.005 --> 00:03:26.571
Having read about it, seen it in a video or tried it one afternoon doesn't count.

00:03:26.871 --> 00:03:30.008
The rule is uncomfortable, and that's why it works.

00:03:30.308 --> 00:03:34.734
Most teams discover they have two or three real pieces and several half done.

00:03:35.034 --> 00:03:36.685
That isn't a bad result.

00:03:36.985 --> 00:03:41.146
It's a precise starting point, and it's what you need to plan without fooling

00:03:41.146 --> 00:03:41.632
yourself.

00:03:42.932 --> 00:03:44.014
Step 3.

00:03:44.314 --> 00:03:46.093
Three pieces, by risk.

00:03:46.643 --> 00:03:51.730
Pick three missing pieces and order them by your product's risk, not by what's most

00:03:51.730 --> 00:03:51.975
fun.

00:03:52.525 --> 00:03:57.545
From the missing ones, pick three and order them by your product's risk, not by what

00:03:57.545 --> 00:03:59.099
you find most interesting.

00:03:59.399 --> 00:04:04.166
If your system has a tool-using agent and there are no injection tests, that goes

00:04:04.166 --> 00:04:05.578
before optimizing costs.

00:04:05.878 --> 00:04:10.641
If a feature with a model is in production with no eval at all, that goes first.

00:04:10.941 --> 00:04:14.927
Ask yourself which failure would hurt you most tomorrow and which piece would have

00:04:14.927 --> 00:04:15.413
caught it.

00:04:15.713 --> 00:04:17.121
That's the first one.

00:04:18.421 --> 00:04:19.433
Step 4.

00:04:19.733 --> 00:04:21.071
A ninety-day plan.

00:04:21.621 --> 00:04:26.639
Build the 90-day plan: one piece per month, each with a verifiable deliverable.

00:04:27.189 --> 00:04:30.930
Assign one piece per month, each with a verifiable deliverable.

00:04:31.230 --> 00:04:33.648
"Learn about evals" isn't a deliverable.

00:04:33.948 --> 00:04:38.188
"A thirty-case eval for the classifier, running in the PR pipeline" is.

00:04:38.488 --> 00:04:40.615
One month per piece looks slow.

00:04:40.915 --> 00:04:44.320
It's realistic if you do it alongside your usual work.

00:04:44.620 --> 00:04:48.442
Put the dates on the calendar and tell someone on your team about the plan.

00:04:48.742 --> 00:04:53.133
A commitment another person knows about gets kept far more often than a private one.

00:04:54.433 --> 00:04:55.562
Step 5.

00:04:55.862 --> 00:04:57.107
Publish evidence.

00:04:57.657 --> 00:05:02.378
Publish evidence: the repo with a README explaining what each part measures, and a

00:05:02.378 --> 00:05:04.393
written case with your own numbers.

00:05:04.943 --> 00:05:07.779
As you finish each piece, leave it visible.

00:05:08.079 --> 00:05:12.574
A repository with a README explaining what each part measures and how to run it.

00:05:12.874 --> 00:05:17.678
A written case with your own numbers: what the problem was, what you measured, what

00:05:17.678 --> 00:05:19.704
you decided and what happened next.

00:05:20.004 --> 00:05:23.884
Use your own or synthetic data, never a client's information.

00:05:24.184 --> 00:05:28.794
That evidence works inside your team, so others adopt what you built, and outside

00:05:28.794 --> 00:05:32.778
it, because it shows applied judgment and not just knowledge of tools.

00:05:34.078 --> 00:05:35.265
Step 6.

00:05:35.565 --> 00:05:36.938
Back to the matrix.

00:05:37.488 --> 00:05:41.484
Update the day 2 matrix and set a date to repeat the self-assessment.

00:05:42.034 --> 00:05:46.553
Open the skills matrix from day two and update it with what you did this month.

00:05:46.853 --> 00:05:50.617
Rows that were at zero or one should now have linked evidence.

00:05:50.917 --> 00:05:55.494
Set a date to repeat the self-assessment three months from now, when the plan ends.

00:05:55.794 --> 00:06:00.000
The specific tools in this series will change, some within a few months.

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:05.268
What holds is the method: define what correct means, measure it repeatably and

00:06:05.268 --> 00:06:06.351
decide with data.

00:06:07.651 --> 00:06:08.757
An example.

00:06:09.307 --> 00:06:12.479
An SDET fills in the checklist at the end of the series.

00:06:12.779 --> 00:06:17.996
Real pieces: the delegation rule, a repo with rules for agents and the TDD cycle

00:06:17.996 --> 00:06:19.039
with guardrails.

00:06:19.339 --> 00:06:21.792
Half done: an eval run by hand.

00:06:22.092 --> 00:06:24.103
Missing: everything else.

00:06:24.403 --> 00:06:28.759
Her product has an assistant with access to customer data and an email tool.

00:06:29.059 --> 00:06:30.478
She orders by risk.

00:06:30.778 --> 00:06:34.148
First month, injection tests on the agent's actions.

00:06:34.448 --> 00:06:38.491
Second month, the eval with a versioned dataset inside the pipeline.

00:06:38.791 --> 00:06:42.253
Third month, traces and a quality alert in production.

00:06:42.553 --> 00:06:46.878
She leaves cost and performance for the following quarter, and writes that down as a

00:06:46.878 --> 00:06:48.268
decision, not an oversight.

00:06:49.568 --> 00:06:51.231
The most common mistakes.

00:06:51.781 --> 00:06:55.139
Marking as done a piece you only saw or tried once.

00:06:55.689 --> 00:06:59.174
Choosing what to build by interest and not by product risk.

00:06:59.724 --> 00:07:02.420
Planning all twelve pieces for the same quarter.

00:07:02.970 --> 00:07:06.108
Publishing evidence with a client's data or details.

00:07:07.408 --> 00:07:09.651
To know it is done, check this.

00:07:10.201 --> 00:07:13.791
The checklist is filled in under the rule that only what runs counts.

00:07:14.341 --> 00:07:16.898
Three pieces are chosen and ordered by risk.

00:07:17.448 --> 00:07:21.050
The ninety-day plan has one verifiable deliverable per month.

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:24.876
The day two matrix is updated and has a review date.

00:07:26.176 --> 00:07:28.072
And where AI fell short.

00:07:28.622 --> 00:07:31.712
It summarizes well and doesn't prioritize.

00:07:32.012 --> 00:07:36.322
Deciding which risk comes first in your product is still a person's job.

00:07:37.622 --> 00:07:39.378
That was day 31.

00:07:39.678 --> 00:07:44.652
The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently

00:07:44.652 --> 00:07:45.660
asked questions.

00:07:46.210 --> 00:07:48.453
This guide closes the series.

00:07:48.753 --> 00:07:50.590
Thanks for making it this far.
